// A pose walk, not a scene. Opened with F10 from the menu.
//
// WHY A SCRIPT AND NOT A KEY THAT CYCLES POSES
//
// The thing worth testing is not the pose, it is the TRANSITION into it -- a
// pose is a still and can be checked in the contact sheet RigPreview writes.
// What only the running game shows is the move: whether an arm takes the short
// way round, whether the coat is left behind and catches up, whether a gesture
// with keyframes holds long enough to be seen before it puts itself away.
//
// So this walks a character through every pose in their .odrig, one page per
// pose, in an order chosen to make the transitions hard: big swings, sign
// changes, a page that moves and poses at once, and a mirrored page so the
// coat can be checked against the wind while the body faces the other way.
//
// It ends by handing over to another character, because a SPEAKER CHANGE is
// its own path through syncRigToDialog -- a second rig gets loaded, the body
// fades, and the pose interpolates from whatever the last one was standing in.
//
// Every pose named here must exist in the speaker's .odrig. A page that names
// one that does not prints "[RIG] no pose" and holds the previous shape, which
// is easy to miss -- so if a page looks like it did nothing, read the console
// before you read the rig.

:: anchor=left size=20 pad=26 enter=type speed=60 line=1.4 pose=idle at=0.5,0.5 scale=0.85

@Signals Officer
**idle.** Arms down, backs of the hands out. This is what everything below
returns to, so it is the one to be sure of first.
---
@Signals Officer
:: pose=hands_up
**hands_up.** The drawing: elbows low and wide, hands level with the hat, palms
turned fully over.

The long way from `idle` — every arm bone changes sign, so if an elbow takes
the long way round through the body, this is the page that shows it.
---
@Signals Officer
:: pose=spread
**spread.** The same palms at waist height. A big fall from arms-up with the
coat following late: watch the hem rather than the hands.
---
@Signals Officer
:: pose=tip_hat
**tip_hat.** One arm up, one staying put. The asymmetric case — the still arm
must actually stay still rather than drifting with the other one.
---
@Signals Officer
:: pose=think
**think.** A small move from `tip_hat`, which is the point of putting them next
to each other: it should not look like a jump.
---
@Signals Officer
:: pose=point at=0.32,0.5
**point.** Moving and posing at once. The body walks left while the arm comes
out — placement runs on its own timer, so this is where the two would fight if
they were going to.
---
@Signals Officer
:: pose=shrug at=0.5,0.5
**shrug.** The only pose here with keyframes on it: shoulders snap up, hold,
drop, and then it puts itself back to `idle` without this script saying so.

Do not click straight through — the whole point is what happens while you wait.
---
@Signals Officer
:: pose=hands_up at=0.66,0.5 flip=1
**mirrored.** The same pose, facing the other way.

The coat must stream the SAME way as it did on the page before: the wind
belongs to the scene, and a body that turns round does not take the weather
with it.
---
@Cryptographer
:: pose=decode at=0.36,0.5 flip=0 scale=1.0
**A different body.** The speaker changed, so a second rig loaded and this one
fades in rather than the first one melting into it.

Both are built on the same measured basis now, so what you are looking for is
not a proportion error — it is whether they read as two different *people*.
Smaller head, narrower shoulders, ordinary hands.
---
@Signals Officer
:: pose=idle at=0.5,0.5 scale=0.85
**idle** again, and back to the first body.

That is the whole walk. F9 hides the labels, F8 mirrors, the right mouse button
drags the body somewhere else, and the wheel scales it.
